  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Write a detailed tutorial on implementing a bounded LRU cache in Python using collections.OrderedDict. Include a complete class, explain get and put behavior, discuss edge cases, and include tests. Continue with a worked example and complexity analysis. Be precise and use meaningful prose and code.\"\n\nWe need produce final answer with detailed tutorial. Need be precise. We can include code, tests, worked example, complexity. Use OrderedDict. Bounded LRU cache: capacity maybe. Need explain get/put: get returns value and moves key to end (most recently used); put updates existing moves to end, if new and capacity exceeded removes oldest (first item). Edge cases: capacity nonnegative? zero capacity? invalid key/value? get missing returns None? Or optional default. Could implement with __getitem__ and __setitem__, or get/put methods. User specifically says explain get and put behavior. Could provide class LRUCache with get and put, and maybe __getitem__/__setitem__ as syntactic sugar. Use OrderedDict: move_to_end, popitem(last=False). Need complete class and tests. Maybe include tests using unittest or pytest. Worked example: simulate operations with capacity 2, show states and outputs. Complexity: O(1) average for get/put if hash table operations O(1); OrderedDict maintains doubly linked list. Space O(capacity). Discuss Python OrderedDict: order insertion, move_to_end O(1), popitem last=False O(1). Edge cases: capacity <1? If allow 0, store no entries; if negative, ValueError. get missing should not alter order. put existing key with same value should still move to end. put new key when full evicts least recently used (first item). If capacity increased? Not necessary; fixed bounded. Could optionally allow resize? Maybe not. But edge case: updating existing key when full doesn't evict. get existing updates recency. get missing doesn't. Use OrderedDict. Could include sentinel for default? Maybe get(key, default=None). If 